In C#, the keyword static is used in several contexts, and it has different meanings depending on where it's used. Here are the main uses of the static keyword in C#:

1. Static Classes:

A static class is a class that cannot be instantiated. It is used to contain only static members (methods, properties, etc.). Static classes are typically used to organize utility methods or constants.

Example:

public static class MathUtils
{
    public static int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
}

You cannot create an instance of a static class:

MathUtils.Add(2, 3); // Valid
var instance = new MathUtils(); // Compilation error

2. Static Members (Methods and Properties):

When a member (method or property) is declared as static, it belongs to the class itself rather than to any instance of the class. This means you can access the static member without creating an instance of the class.

Example:

public class MyClass
{
    public static int StaticProperty { get; set; }

    public static void StaticMethod()
    {
        // ...
    }
}

Usage:

MyClass.StaticProperty = 10;
MyClass.StaticMethod();

3. Static Fields:

A static field is a variable that belongs to the class rather than to any instance of the class. All instances of the class share the same static field.



